2036. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 16227562
T1  - Loss of taste during treatment with synthetic antithyroid drugs: Physiopathogenesis and treatment with zinc sulfate
T3  - AGUEUSIE DUE AUX ANTI-THYROIDIENS DE SYNTHESE: ETUDE DE LA PHYSIOPATHOGENIE ET TRAITEMENT PAR LE SULFATE DE ZINC. UNE OBSERVATION
A1  - Emy Ph.
A1  - Pinsard D.
A1  - Bentata J. 
Y1  - 1985//
N2  - Antithyroid drugs can be responsible for taste dysfunction. This dysgueusia is due to a decreased saliva zinc level relative to the chelating action of antithyroid drugs. Locally a decreased zinc blood level alters human gustin synthesis. Gustin is a zinc bound protein which exerts an action on taste buds. The reported case suggests that zinc sulfate orally administered is able to correct the zinc imbalance responsible for taste dysfunction without antithyroid treatment being withdrawn.
