2107. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 11076529
T1  - Gross behavioral and physiological effects of hallucinogens in conscious restrained monkeys
A1  - Wilson M.C.
A1  - Bedford J.A.
A1  - Davis W.M. 
Y1  - 1981//
N2  - Intravenous injections of 3,4-methylenedioxyamphetamine (MDA), 2,4,5-trimethoxyamphetamine (TMA-2), N,N-dimethyltryptamine (DMT) or psilocybin were given to conscious, chair restrained cynomolgus monkeys (Macaca fascicularis). Body temperature, heart rate and respiratory rate were monitored. General activity and reactivity, visual behaviour, pupil size, motor function and other behavioral changes were observed at intervals after dosing. All test agents except DMT produced visual and/or motor changes suggestive of hallucinatory phenomena. All doses of each agent disrupted normal behavior, i.e., caused a refusal to accept fruit. Only TMA-2 caused intense stereotypy, tremors and convulsions. All except TMA-2 tended to depress reactivity. Mydriasis and tachycardia were consistent effects of all four agents, whereas changes in respiratory rate and body temperature varied between drugs. The two indolealkylamines caused bizarre posturing. Thus, the pattern of physiological and behavioral responses was not constant across these four agents. There did not appear to be a distinctive behavioral response among all monkeys that would consistently reflect perceptual alterations that may be induced by these compounds.
