2113. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 11009664
T1  - Mushroom poisoning
A1  - Banahan Jr. B.F. 
Y1  - 1981//
N2  - Of seven major categories of mushroom poisoning dicussed, the one most commonly encountered by the physician is simple gastrointestinal irritation that responds well to fluid and electrolyte replacement. The more serious poisonings by the amatoxins and gyromitrin are characterized by a delay in onset. The deadly amatoxin poisoning has a third stage which is an apparent recovery that may lull the unwary physician into failure to react properly. Thiotic acid, penicillin r318w1 =N-{1-[2-(4-ethyl-4,5-dihydro-5-oxo-1H-tetrazol-l-yl)ethyl ]-4-(methoxymethyl)-4-piperidinyl}-N-phenyl glucose and good supportive treatment seems to have greatly improved prognosis in amatoxin poisoning. Symptomatic treatment is generally adequate for the mushroom poisonings other than amatoxin and gyromitrin. The possibility that research and experience with rocket fuel intoxiation has provided us with an antidote for gyromitrin poisoning exists. Pyridoxine, which has effectively cured workers of poisoning by hydrazine rocket fuels, should be tried in poisoning by the gyromitra. The use of atropine in mushroom poisoning is seldom appropriate and in some cases the toxicity is made worse by such anticholergics. Atropine should be used only to counteract observed muscarinic effects, and then in only moderate doses.
