2126. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 10065610
T1  - Pharmacological characteristics of tetrahydrocannabinol seizure susceptible rabbits
A1  - Consroe P.
A1  - Fish B.S. 
Y1  - 1980//
N2  - Rabbits (thc/thc) from our closed rabbit colony [Uaz: (NZW:thc)] exhibit nonfatal behavioral convulsions when given low intravenous (IV) doses of psychoactive cannabinoids. DELTA9 Tetrahydrocannabinol (DELTA9THC), 11-hydroxy DELTA9THC, DELTA8THC, 11hydroxy DELTA8THC, DELTA9THC-1-morpholinobutyrate, nabilone, 11-hydroxycannabinol (11OH-CBN) and hexahydro-CBN are effective at .1-1 mg/kg while 15 or 20 mg/kg of CBN is required. Latencies to seizure onset (.5-6 minutes), seizure durations (.2-3 minutes) and/or occurrence and duration of seizure endpoints (mydriasis, thrashing, head tuck, limb extensions, nystagmus) are dose-related following DELTA9THC administration (.1-.9 mg/kg IV). Nonpsychoactive cannabinoids (8-20 mg/kg, IV of cannabidiol (CBD), cannabicyclol, cannabichromene, 11-nor-9-carboxy DELTA8THC), cannabinoid vehicle (10% Tween 80/saline), and other hallucinogenic drugs (.1-40 mg/kg, IV, of LSD, mescaline, psilocybin, phencyclidine, methamphetamine) do not produce convulsions. DELTA9THC-induced convulsions are prevented (in nontoxic doses) by carbamazepine, diazepam, phenytoin and CBD, but not by naloxone, reserpine, or SKF525A pretreatments. Also, tolerance to CBN (15 mg/kg IV) and DELTA9THC (.1 mg/kg IV) and asymmetrical crosstolerance from CBN to DELTA9THC occurs. It is hypothesized that the thc/thc rabbit represents a unique biological model of marihuana psychoactivity.
