2132. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 11231559
T1  - Practical approach to drug addiction
T3  - ATTITUDES PRATIQUES DEVANT LA TOXICOMANIE
A1  - Curtet F. 
Y1  - 1980//
N2  - The author argues that although it is difficult to find the time and energy to deal with other categories of patients if one is having to treat drug addicts, it is wrong to suppose that they should be treated only by psychiatrists or former addicts. In fact, drug addiction is not a disease but a response to a malaise which has its roots in the addict's social, economic and political background. In order to tackle drug addiction, one must take account of multiple factors and to cooperate with all the various people who have been trained to deal with this problem: thus it is also important to seek the advice of the legal profession. The author, however, argues against therapy which involves compulsory military service or imprisonment since this is generally useless, though some addicts may benefit from discussing their problems with a therapist in prison. The press tends to exaggerate the extent of drug addiction at schools and universities. Post-cure institutions, often rural, offer the ex-addict the chance to get away from his normal environment, thus to free himself from the physical and psychological factors which had engendered his former addiction. The treatment of cocaine abuse is like that of amphetamine abuse, that is, one has to treat the anxiety, depression and especially the hallucinatory reactions. Whilst doctors dealing with drug addiction need to have concrete advice, one should not forget about a theoretical analysis of the very special relationship between the addict and his therapist.
