2133. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 11227134
T1  - Hallucinogenic activity of 2 mushrooms of the genus Psilocybe from Czechoslovakia
T3  - HALLUZINOGENE WIRKUNGEN ZWEIER HUTPILZE DER GATTUNG PSILOCYBE TSCHECHOSLOWAKISCHER HERKUNFT
A1  - Auert G.
A1  - Dolezal V.
A1  - Hausner M.
A1  - Semerdzieva M. 
Y1  - 1980//
N2  - The presence of the psychotropic substance psilocybin in the toadstools Psilocybe semilanceata and P. mairei was confirmed by 3 auto-experiments. The psychovegetative effects in humans corresponded to the effects of the pure substances. However, the former demonstrated a shorter latency period prior to the first signs of intoxication which all in all were weaker and of shorter duration than those produced by the pure substances. Also, the toadstool extracts produced fewer vegetative complaints, less 'color' and more contemplative effects. There was a quantitative difference between the effects produced by the two toadstool extracts which may have been due to unequal doses. The amount of psilocybin present in the P. semilanceata extract seemed to be higher than was indicated by the chromatogram - but this also seemed likely in the case of a test chromatogram done with the pure substance. Furthermore, it is possible that there are other, hitherto unknown substances in the extract which may inhibit or stimulate the effect of the alkaloid. When compared with LSD or mescalin, psilocybin demonstrates effects of shorter duration with fewer psychovegetative side- and after-effects. Where the effects of all three substances are apparently the same, it may be possible to make a distinction between psilocybin and LSD inasmuch that the former does not given rise to the known phases of anxietous delirium and oscillation between oneiroid and reality as produced by LSD. Of course, with psilocyloin, perception - particularly 'colorful hearing' - is more intense.
