2150. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 4213423
T1  - Mutagenic side effects of drugs in humans
T3  - ERBGUTSCHADIGENDE ARZNEIMITTEL NEBENWIRKUNGEN BEIM MENSCHEN?
A1  - Gebhart E. 
Y1  - 1974//
N2  - Drug investigations carried out to date with respect to possible mutagenic effects have involved relatively few of the many substances available. In a number of cases, the examination of the chromosomes of patients treated chemotherapeutically has considerably increased the reliability of the findings made. It can be stated that in humans, the alkylating cytostatic agents (e.g. nitrogen mustard, cyclophosphamide, triaziquone, tretamine, tris(1 aziridinyl)phosphine oxide (TEPA), thioTEPA and busulfane), a number of nucleic acid metabolites (e.g. 5 fluordesoxyuridin, arabinosyd cytosine, 6 mercaptopurine, propylthiouracil, hydroxyurea) and the antibiotica with a cytostatic effect (e.g. mitomycin C, daunomycin = rubidomycin, bleomycin, streptonigrin = bruneomycin) are certainly mutagenic. The following agents are already strongly suspected of having a mutagenic effect: chlorambucil, azathioprine (at high concentrations), 6 azauridin, actinomycin D, phleomycin, anticonvulsants and psilocybin. Positive findings in individual examinations, or in individual test systems (with simultaneous negative findings in other examinations) were established for methotrexate, azauridin, caffeine (which is today considered as comutagenic), streptomycin, certain phenothiazines, LSD, 8 hydroquinoline sulphate and cyclamate. Studies of penicillin, cotrimoxazole, chloramphenicol, cyproheptadine, chlordiazepoxide, lithium salts, diazepam, oral contraceptives (ovulation inhibitors) and phenylbutazone have to date revealed no definite evidence of a mutagenic effect in humans.
KW  - *adverse drug reaction
KW  - *chromosome damage
KW  - etiology
KW  - *mutagenesis
