2163. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 9132239
T1  - Psilocybin neuropsychopharmacology in rabbits
A1  - Guest J.
A1  - Consroe P. 
Y1  - 1979//
N2  - Quantitative electroencephalographic (EEG) and behavioral effects of intravenous psilocybin (1 and 3 mg/kg) and saline were studied in rabbits using a cross-over design with 72 hours between treatments. Psilocybin produced decreases in cortical EEG voltage output, theta and alpha frequencies, and increases in delta and beta energies, concomitant with biphasic alterations in standing and decrements in grooming, rearing and locomotion behaviors. The available data of laboratory animal-human correlation suggest that this rabbit paradigm is suitable for study of psilocybin neuropsychopharmacology.
