2181. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 10231877
T1  - Abuse of psychoactive substances: Tolerance and dependence
A1  - Hoes M.J.A.J.M. 
Y1  - 1979//
N2  - The literature is surveyed of tolerance for and dependence on psychoactive compounds, including opiates, hypno-sedatives, psychostimulants, and hallucinogenic drugs. Epidemiologically, misuse of these durgs occurs on a grand scale, the more, so because some psychotherapeutic drugs are also involved. Tolerance and dependence do not necessarily develop synchronously for each durg, and tolerance may differentially develop for each effect of a certain drug. The great number of normal persons who are found among drug-dependent people stresses dependence as a disease sui generis. There are three type of pathophysiological models for tolerance and dependence: hypothetical, mathematical and specific cellular adaptation. The drug can exert their effect by receptors for natural agonists, by receptors for well known neurotransmitters or by way of non-specific elements of neural membranes. In the last two chapters of this review, clinical applications of the data in diagnosis and treatment of dependence on and tolerance for the four classes of drugs, are discussed. This review will be published in two parts. The first part ends with the discussion of hypotheses for tolerance and dependence.
