2211. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 9025707
T1  - Patterns of brain monoamine activity and aggressive behavior
A1  - Daruna J.H. 
Y1  - 1978//
N2  - Research bearing on the question of how treatments that alter the brain monoamines influence the intraspecies aggression of rodents is critically examined. An attempt is made to identify the patterns of monoamine activity that accompany the induction of aggressive behavior, and to determine whether distinct patterns of activity are specific to the various experimental paradigms of aggressive behavior. It is evident from the literature that many critical questions remain to be answered. Nevertheless, it appears that 3 different patterns of monoamine activity are associated with enhanced intraspecies aggression. No support is found for any exclusive association between specific patterns and the different paradigms of intraspecies aggression. Moreover, the widespread notion that serotonin is inhibitory for aggression is not consistent with the bulk of the evidence. These conclusions are discussed in terms of possible neurochemical differences between attack and defense components of aggression. In closing, suggestions for future research are offered.
