2215. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 8275570
T1  - Drugs and smoking during pregnancy
T3  - DROGUES ET TABAC PENDANT LA GROSSESSE
A1  - Tescher M.
A1  - Rudelin D.
A1  - Maloux G. 
Y1  - 1978//
N2  - The effects of narcotics, alcohol and smoking on the course of pregnancy were considered in turn. As regards narcotics the opiates commonly provoke fetal hypotrophy and an abstinence syndrome in the child; the hallucinogens seem to increase the number of spontaneous abortions and congenital malformations, especially in the central nervous systems; Cannabis extracts however do not seem to have any repercussion on gestation in the human species. Chronic alcoholic intoxication is said to delay growth in utero, provoke a mental deficit and a dysmorphic syndrome involving mainly the face and eyes. Smoking apparently is mainly responsible for a certain degree of hypertrophy and an increase in perinatal mortality.
