2219. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 8179066
T1  - Genetic aspects of pharmacology
T3  - GENETISCHE ASPEKTE DER PHARMAKOLOGIE
A1  - Goedde H.W. 
Y1  - 1977//
N2  - Following application of medication, the responsiveness, therapeutic results as well as side effects can vary considerably. Individual differences in the type of reaction can be genetically conditioned. If quantitative values - such as for example the concentration of a substance in the blood - shows variations in dosage/efficacy for different individuals in the sense of a bimodal distribution, then genetic methods can be used to check whether these individual differences are produced by exogenous factors or were inherited. So-called inherited values can be calculated; studying the individual's family can provide information regarding inheritance. When considering pharmacogenetic reactions of this nature, the following consequences should be kept in mind: development and clinical testing of new pharmaceutical preparations, their application in various populations, prophylaxis, prognosis, and genetic counselling of patients. In the primary sense, the study of the interrelationship between influence of inheritance and environment (the latter also includes pharmacotherpy) is the task of ecogenetics. Intoxication via poisons and 'pollutants' as well as the effect of mutagenic substances and of industrial allergens are also important consideration for preventive and industrial medicine.
