2230. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 8009853
T1  - A contribution to pathophysiology of so called frontal intermittent delta activity in EEG
A1  - Kolarik J.
A1  - Waberzinek G.
A1  - Penickova V. 
Y1  - 1977//
N2  - Frontal intermittent rhythmic delta activity (FIRDA) has been described both in brain tumors of various localizations and in cerebrovascular affections, encephalopathies of various etiology, infections including tuberculous meningitis and basilar arachnoiditis, in degenerative brain diseases, after prefrontal leukotomies, and even in purely functional states. Considering the controversy about the anatomical substrate and genesis of these functional manifestations we tried to differentiate the possibly involved systems on pharmacological grounds. A psilocybin dose of 0.16 mg/kg caused desynchronization of the EEG pattern, suppression of pathological EEG activity, mainly distant rhythms and hypersynchronic epileptic activity. To find out whether it is not only desynchronization that participates nonspecifically in the suppression of these activities we used three substances desynchronizing EEG pattern by way of affecting various mediator systems: psilocybin with antiserotoninergic, physostigmine with cholinergic, and amphetamine with adrenergic effects. Psilocybin reduced remarkably the total time occurrence of FIRDA and number of individual FIRDA episodes both during hyperventilation and normal breathing by 60-70%. Physostigmine practically did not influence any of the followed indicators. Amphetamine, on the contrary, was followed by a prolongation of the total time of FIRDA occurrence, even if the number of episodes remained uninfluenced, i.e. the episodes were rather long. This supports the finding that some raphe neurons receive catecholamine inputs. Psilocybin evidently suppresses the socalled distant EEG rhythms specifically by its antiserotoninergic effect and this suppression cannot be due to some nonspecific effect of desynchronization. The assumed anatomical substrate of the serotoninergic system is the serotoninergic raphe system of the brain stem, particularly of the midbrain with its rostral projections via medial forebrain bundle and through medial thalamic nuclei towards prefrontal and orbitofrontal cortex, and basal forebrain area. These structures are part of the interoreflex interoceptive interomotor circuit. It is our opinion that both the mechanism of herniation in intracranial hypertension and the nature of the terminal blood supply of raphe nuclei in cerebrovascular lesions as well as other pathophysiological mechanisms (such as distension of periaquaeductal grey matter) bring about lesion of raphe nuclei inducing in this way FIRDA manifestation in the EEG pattern. For the genesis of FIRDA in cerebellar tumors we postulate a central role of the noradrenergic locus coeruleus, which on the one hand sends projections to the cerebellum while, on the other hand, powerful components from the more ventromedial parts of the locus coeruleus pass into the raphe complex, especially into the nuclei raphe pontis and nucleus raphe dorsalis. This facilitates intimate contrary interaction of the serotoninergic and noradrenergic systems indicated by our results obtained from FIRDA suppression after psilocybin and its facilitation after amphetamine.
