2238. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 8112581
T1  - Hallucinogenic drugs. Their ophthalmological side effects
T3  - LES HALLUCINOGENES. INCIDENCES OPHTALMOLOGIQUES
A1  - Verin Ph.
A1  - Vildy A.
A1  - Maurin J.F.
A1  - Hubert G. 
Y1  - 1977//
N2  - The main hallucinogenic drugs are hashish, amphetamines, lysergic acid, mescaline and psylocybin. Most of these substances have long been known in Africa, Asia and Latin America, although their use in Europe and the United-States is a fairly recent phenomenon. The hallucinogens belong either to the indole or phenylethylamine groups, except for cannabis which is different in its chemical makeup. The various effects brought about by their absorption form a semiological group, which can vary according to the substance, persons or doses involved, but the effects nevertheless remain within the limits of several characteristic syndromes. The principal phenomenons created by hallucinogenic drugs are: modifications in mood, affectivity and thought patterns, together with an alteration in consciousness, confusion and sometimes deliriousness. At the sensorial level, disturbances in perception, illusions and hallucinations are frequent. The most spectacular sensorial effects created by the hallucinogens are, without doubt, those that are visual. Intensity of colors is heightened and an impression of vision stereoscopic is frequent. Perception of distances is modified and leads to a distortion of outlines and an illusion of movement. These simple or complex illusions can be completed by actual hallucinations which bring about a more complete alteration in the field of consciousness. Finally, the frequent occurrence of synesthesia must be noted, although this is not an effect specific to hallucinogenic intoxication. Hallucinogenic substances not only transform the often dull universe of man into extraordinary illusions, they unfortunately also lead to ophthalmological complications, some of which can be serious. The following are the most frequent: Pupillary modifications, constant but varying with the toxic substance and which, when associated with changes in ocular pressure, can provoke glaucoma in some cases; Vascular retinal accidents with amphetamines; Finally, although hashish does not cause optical neuritis, L.S.D. does, and there is still a doubt concerning its innocuousness to the products of fecondation in intoxicated mothers. Other complications can arise which we have called functional complications since these are closer to visual phenomenons than to actual ophthalmological problems. Moreover, what also must be mentioned are those complications not specific of hallucinogens but common to all drugs, such as fungal endophthalmitis secondary to embolism originating from the place of injection. The possibility of ophthalmological complications arising in intoxicated subjects must therefore not be underestimated and it would seem important to preceed with a systematic ophthalmological checkup in such cases.
