2245. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 8094890
T1  - Serotonin and sexual behaviour in female rats. Effects of hallucinogenic indolealkylamines and phenylethylamines
A1  - Everitt B.J.
A1  - Fuxe K. 
Y1  - 1977//
N2  - The effects of a number of hallucinogenic indolealkylamines and phenylethylamines on sexual receptivity in female rats have been studied. Preliminary experiments on extensor reflexes (which are highly dependent on serotonin (5-HT) receptor activity) indicated that the drugs possess marked 5-HT receptor agonist properties. All compounds increase sexual receptivity in low doses but inhibit this behaviour in high doses. 5-HT neurones are known to subserve inhibitory mechanisms in the control of receptivity. It is suggested that the 5-HT agonists used enhance receptivity by preferentially stimulating presynaptic 5-HT receptors and inhibit receptivity by stimulating postsynaptic 5-HT receptors. Microiontophoretic and microelectrode recording data consistent with this hypothesis are discussed.
