2252. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 7166231
T1  - Hypothesis: Migraine, a central biochemical dysnociception
A1  - Sicuteri F. 
Y1  - 1976//
N2  - This detailed article deals with the biochemical aspects and pharmacology of essential headache (EH). Migraine is taken as the prototype of EH and is considered as a dysfunction of nociception, which is called dysnociception. In fact, migraine and other types of EH are the most common dysnociceptions. Chemical and experimental evidence are reviewed for evidence that supports the author's belief that neurotransmitters, especially the monoamines, are implicated in the central mechanism of pain perceptions. Parachlorophenylalanine (5 HT) depletion produces pain hypersensitivity. Increase in brain 5 HT produces a morphine like effect and will potentiate the analgesic effect of subliminal doses of morphine. Increase of 5 HT also counteracts the effect of reserpine administration which otherwise produces a loss of the analgesic effect of morphine. A central congenital biochemical dysfunction of pain perception is postulated for man since normal volunteers with reduced 5 HT turnover did not complain of spontaneous pain, except a few who had headache. However migraine sufferers had precipitation of attack and individuals with other forms of EH had exacerbation of their headaches with reduced 5 HT turnover. The episodic attacks of migraine are manifestations of the endogenous biologic rhythms (circadian or infradian) with associated variations in nociception. Other biorhythms which influence EH (including migraine in some instances) are catamenia, sleepiness, wakefulness, psychomotor stress, sexual excitation and coitus. All these are discussed separately, including fasting, insulin effects, and the increase in sexual excitation when PCPA is given as contrasted with the reduction in sexual desire in many cases of EH. The pharmacologic effects of Methysergide (UML 491), Lysergic acid diethylamide (LSD 25), the serotonin precursors (tryptophan and 5 hydroxytryptophan) and the dopamine precursors (levo dopa) are discussed in the light of the concept of the biochemical and pharmacologic mechanism acting on a congenital disturbance of nociception. The author points out that the vascular theory of causation of migraine seemed initially simple but eventually proved complex. He feels that the central monoamine theory is more convincing and reconsideration of conventional diagnostic assumptions is necessary. (Voris - Chicago, Ill.)
