2313. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 5191019
T1  - Principles of antidepressant therapy and results obtained with Hungarian thymoleptics in hospitalized psychiatric patients
A1  - Vajda L. 
Y1  - 1974//
N2  - The treatment of depressions is a complex task, the components of which are pharmaco-, somato- and psychotherapy. The administration of thymoleptics is the basis of the treatment of endogenous and involutional depressions. Thymoleptics and somatotherapy are applied in organic and symptomatic depressions. After improvement of the symptoms psychotherapy has to be used in both groups as adjuvant therapy. Psychotherapy is the basis of the therapy in psychogenous depressions which should be completed with thymoleptic treatment. Of the thymoleptics Melipramin relieves endogenous depressions but it controls inhibition and mild anxiety as well. Improvement of depressive mood is more expressed and occurs within a shorter period after the application of Teperin. It relieves agitated restlessness and anxiety and has a significant sedative and hypnotic effect, too. Sapilent relieves both depressive mood and milder anxiety. Tisercin is the drug of high value in relieving anxiety beside exerting significant neuroleptic and mild depression relieving activity. In agitated anxiety it is Teperin; in inhibited anxiety, Melipramin or Sapilent that may be administered successfully. Good responses were obtained with Melipramin and Teperin primarily in endogenous and involutional depressions. Sapilent proved to be effective mainly in senile arteriosclerotic patients and in psychogenous depressions. Combination of the previously mentioned drugs with Tisercin is required in patients suffering from serious anxiety.
