2315. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 6056182
T1  - Headache biochemistry and pharmacology
A1  - Sicuteri F. 
Y1  - 1974//
N2  - Human and animal evidence point to an involvement of monoamines in the cerebral mechanism of pain. A normal cerebral turnover of 5 HT is necessary for a physiological balance of pain function. A 5 HT deficiency in the brain lowers the threshold for pain in animals and man. During pharmacologically induced deficiency of 5 HT using fenclonine, spontaneous pain of a central type may arise in headache sufferers. In attacks of migraine some primitive functions, such as appetite, nausea, vomiting, sleep, sex and thermoregulation are affected. These functions are partly influenced by monoamines. Pain, the most typical symptom of migraine headache, may be one of these fundamental functions involved in the attack. Thus, pain may not be peripheral, i.e. vascular in nature, but central or cerebral and the whole headache syndrome may be considered a unique mechanism, that is a genetically determined derangement of the metabolism of the brain stem monoamines in general, and of 5 HT in particular. If a deficiency of cerebral 5 HT is envisaged, a state of 'central supersensitivity' must also be postulated. If this new theory is accepted, the term 'vascular', in which most essential headaches including migraine are comprised, must be deemed only of historical significance, with dilatation and pulsation of vessels as a complementary but not essential component. In the light of this central biochemical theory, the precipitating or interrupting action of drugs and spontaneous events may be interpreted in terms of brain stem monoamine metabolism. The therapeutic mechanism of classical drugs, like ergotamine, antiserotonin and more recently 5 HT precursors, e.g. 1 tryptophan and 1,5 hydroxytryptophan, must therefore be reconsidered.
