2336. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 4180307
T1  - A novel experience with panaeolus. A case study from Hawaii
A1  - Pollock S.H. 
Y1  - 1974//
N2  - An ethnobotanical case study is presented which for the first time documents the employment of Panaeolus cyanescens Berkeley and Broome as a hallucinogen. Hawaii is a new locality being cited for the occurrence of this species and while it does not seem to have been used in native Hawaiian culture, it is actively utilized by part of the American subculture present in Hawaii. A subcultural employment of this mushroom in other parts of the world would not be surprising. Since many types of mushrooms other than P. cyanescens occur in Hawaii, a detailed mycological and chemical investigation would be of great value. Many species of mushrooms superficially resemble the Panaeoli and occur in the same habitats. Although there is an excellent color picture available of P. campanulatus (the type species of the genus), one must be very cautious in selecting any mushrooms for consumption remembering that many are deadly. Furthermore, many of the species of Panaeoli will probably have no psychotropic activity at all according to various studies completed so far.
