2337. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 5207597
T1  - 5,000 bad trips
A1  - Abruzzi W. 
Y1  - 1974//
N2  - The author defined a bad trip as a drug induced, emotional condition ranging from an acute panic state or severe anxiety episode on the one hand, to a frankly psychotic experience on the other. Serious long range psychiatric residua after unhappy, frightening, or psychotic drug experiences are more common than frequently reported. Those with histories of emotional disturbances, and especially bad trips, should avoid intoxicants including alcohol and marijuana. As already accomplished in some progressive countries, amphetamines should be outlawed except for certain, very few carefully controlled cases. LSD is an impressively powerful chemical, capable in a statistically significant number of cases of producing psychotic postures, (temporary or permanent) flashbacks, seizures, and, perhaps, suicide. Bad trips, when possible, should be treated with reassurance, psychologically oriented counseling, and compassion. Unless the time and personnel situation absolutely prohibits, the treatment of 'bad trips' with medicines should be discouraged. Thorazine (chlorpromazine) should never be used for the treatment of a 'bad trip'. Emergency room and psychiatric hospital procedure should be revised so as to make them less judgmental, less frightening and coercive, more compassionate, and more attractive and acceptable to young people.
