2348. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 4176017
T1  - Vocalization: A characteristic cannabis induced behavior in the rat?
A1  - Jarbe T.U.C.
A1  - Henriksson B.G. 
Y1  - 1973//
N2  - After treatment with tetrahydrocannabinol (THC, >= 5 mg/kg, IP), most rats vocalize in a characteristic manner when touched. In general, vocalization is considered a sign of distress or pain. It therefore seemed of interest to study the effects of some psychotropic drugs on the cannabis induced vocalization behavior. It was found that tranquillizers, morphine, and tacrine (THA) inhibited the THC induced vocalization. Fenitron was found ineffective in this respect. Vocalization was not noted after injections with 2 other naturally occurring cannabinoids, cannabinol and cannabidiol. The possibility that THC has aversive properties in rats is discussed.
