2361. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 4100494
T1  - Toxic factors in schizophrenia
A1  - Ridges A.P. 
Y1  - 1973//
N2  - Hallucinogens such as LSD, mescaline, psilocyn and STP, which induce schizophreniclike symptoms, all possess either a methyl or a methoxy radical. Methylation of serotonin results in the formation of another hallucinogenic substance (bufotenin) and the methoxylation of melatonin in yet another hallucinogen (10 methoxyharmalan). In schizophrenia one may also observe an abnormal configuration of the S protein; a special gammaglobulin named taraxein (which in the form of antibody becomes bound to the nuclei of nerve cells); and a rehomelanine (dimethoxyphenylalanine) derived from catecholamines. The author found this substance in the urine of a highly significant proportion of schizophrenics.
