2574. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 292122125
T1  - Eeg changes after psilocybin in organic brain lesions
A1  - Kolarik J. 
Y1  - 1971//
N2  - A study was made of the effect of psilocybine in a mean dose of 0.16 mg/kg on the EEG picture in 54 subjects with organic brain lesions of various localization and etiology, mostly verified neurosurgically. Results: the most typical finding after psilocybine was a desynchronization in 79.6% of patients (in 100% of parietal lobe lesions, in 801 of frontal lobe lesions, in 75% of temporal lobe lesions and in 33.3% of occipital lobe lesions). The desynchronization after psilocybine was of a peculiar character in the EEG with focal and diffuse pathologic activity. The extent and intensity of the slow activity were most suppressed in frontal and parietal lesions, and to a lesser extent in temporal and occipital lesions. The psychotomimetic effects of psilocybine correlated with the EEG desynchronization. The hallucinogenic effect was absent in 90.9% of cases without desynchronization following psilocybine. The visual hallucinogenic effect and EEG desynchronization (Kolarik et al. 1966) were least frequent in occipital lobe lesions. In occipital lesions suppression of EEG reactivity was the greatest (alpha blocking reaction and photostimulation). Diffuse epileptic activity of subcortical origin was strongly suppressed by psilocybine, while conversely focal primary cortical epileptic activity increased in several recordings depending upon the degree of desynchronization, especially in parietofrontal lesions. Four functional levels within the CNS are considered from which a generalized desynchronization could be evoked: (1) The level of the pontine reticular formation whose significance is made evident in the studies of rhombencephalic sleep. (2) The level of the reticular ascending activating system (RAAS). Most authors assume that hallucinogenic drugs act precisely at this level of the brain stem reticular formation. (3) The functional depression of the recruiting system at the level of the medial thalamus (Monnier 1959). (4) Hyperexcitability of the cortical neurons after psilocybine. The highest values of psilocybine were found in the neocortex, thalamus and certain layers of the hippocampus whilst the values were low in the hypothalamus, the nuclei of the socalled extrapyramidal motor system and the reticular formation.
