2575. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 292122123
T1  - Different reaction of focal and diffuse epileptic eeg activity to psilocybin
A1  - Kolarik J. 
Y1  - 1971//
N2  - In 30 epileptic patients the influence of psilocybine (average dose 0.16 mg/kg) on the epiletic EEG tracing was studied. Before the adinistration of psilocybine (Ps) pathologic EEG changes were present in all 30 patients. Ps provoked a generalized EEG desynchronization in 90% (27 patients). Focal epileptic activity (present in 70% i.e. 21 patients before Ps) increased after Ps in 23.3% (7 patients) and remained unchanged or was attenuated in 46.6% (14 patients). Diffuse epileptic activity present in 63.3% (i.e. 17 patients) before Ps persisted in only 101 (3 patients) after Ps. Diffuse epileptic activity of subcortical origin was most strongly suppressed by Ps (a decreased incidence of paroxysmal bilateral synchronous spike and wave complexes as well as of interparoxysmal continuous diffuse slow activity, and other subcortical discharges of a different etiology). Desynchronization occuired in 100% of parietal lobe lesions, in 80% of frontal lobe lesions, in 75% of temporal lobe lesions and in 33.3% of occipital lobe lesions (Kolarik 1967). The incidence of focal epileptic activity after Ps was similar: it was most frequent in parietal lobe lesions (751 in comparison with 62.5% before Ps), and was noted in 50% of frontal lobe lesions, in 37.5% of temporal lobe lesions and only in 33.3% of occipital lobe lesions. It is suggested that hyperexcitability of cortical neurons (Gastaut 1953) produced by Ps is analogous with their pathologic irritability giving rise to the focal epileptic manifestations. The role of the depression of the mediothalamic recruiting system after Ps as demonstrated experimentally by Monnier (1959) and its special action in 2 opposing directions is noteworthy. On the one hand, simultaneously with the depression of the recruiting system the subcortical epileptic activities and other slow wave activities, subcortical in origin, are suppressed. On the other hand, the depression of the recruiting system localizes cortical desynchronization predominantly in a frontoparietal recruiting system (Whitteridge and Walsh 1963), thus accentuating the focal epileptic activity in this localization. This may be due not only to the induction of cortical excitation by the depression of the mediothalamic recruiting system but also to a direct exciting effect of psilocybine on cortical elements.
