2595. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 290040319
T1  - Proprioceptive hallucinations and accentuation or manifestation of neurologic symptomatology after administration of psilocybine to persons with cerebral lesions
T3  - Propklozeptlve slnnestauschumibn und akzentuiefhng hzw manifestwkrden neuroirgiscueii svmpixjmatik unter psilocybin bei hlrngeschadtoten versuchspersonen
A1  - Dubansky B.
A1  - Vyhnankova M.
A1  - Setlik L. 
Y1  - 1968//
N2  - In view of the fact that proprioception, as a feedback component, is an integral factor in all forms of motor activity, and that propiioceptive hallucinations are, together with the optic stimulation syndrome, the commonest effects of psilocybine, an investigation of a possible correlation between proprioceptive hallucinations and motor performance was conducted with 99 patients having brain lesions of various etiology and localization. A single dose (0.15 mg/kg) of psilocybine was administered orally or s.c. The following neurological signs (percentage incidence in brackets) were evaluated in relation to the occurrence of proprioceptive hallucinations: Accentuation of paretic phenomena of central origin (84%); manifestation of paretic phenomena previously absent (45degree/); dysarthria (5Ti); accentuation ot some second signal disorders (disturbance of expressive speeck, amnestic aphasia, agraphia, apraxia, stereoagnosia) (68degree4); disorders of neuromuscular coordination (534); motor irritative phenomena such as tremor and myoclonus (2ffu) and provocation of epileptic phenomena (22degree4). Of 48 patients with accentuation of paretic signs, 39 (8) had proprioceptive sensations (significant correlation: x2corr. = 6.46; p< 0.05). For manifestation of paretic phenomena (16 of the 39 patients initially free from paresis) 14 of the patients had concomitant proprioceptive hallucinations (x2corr.=3.72; p<0.05). For accentuation or manifestation of dysarthria (x2=s3.17; p<0.05), for occurrence of coordination disturbances (x2=3.93; p < 0.05) and for deterioration of second signal phenomena related to motor activity (x2=5.29; p<0.05) the findings were similar. No statistically significant correlation be established between trc;vsui, epileptic phenomena etc. induced by psilocybine and the presence of proprioceptive hallucinations because the numbers were too small. Reference is made to other publications, and further studies, making use of electromyography, are proposed.
