2596. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 290040312
T1  - Differences in reaction after administration of psilocybin in persons suffering from cerebral lesions of different localization
T3  - Unterschiede in der reaktionsweise von psilocybin bei hibngeschadigten versuchspersonen bezogen auf die lokalisation der lasjon
A1  - Dubansky B.
A1  - Vyhnankova M. 
Y1  - 1968//
N2  - The possibility of difference in susceptibility to psilocybine between normal subjects and patients with organic brain damage of various localizations was investigated. A single dose of 0.15 mg/kg psilocybine was given orally or s.c. to 99 patients and 12 normal subjects. It was already known that the effect of psychotomimetic (hallucinogenic) agents is appreciably less in brain damaged or psychotic patients than in normal persons; this was confirmed with psilocybine, especially as regards the optic stimulation syndrome. More detailed analysis showed that the reduced optic effect, and in most of these cases also the reduced total psychotomimetic effect, was to some degree correlated with the nature of the brain lesion, the presence of an elevated intracranial pressure and the age and sex of the patient. No influence of the administration route was discernible. A notable and statistically highly significant (chi2 32.71; p < 0.01) correlation was found between the reduced optic effect of psilocybine and the localization of the brain lesion. In patients with lesions of the occipital lobe and the adjacent temporal and parietal regions (33 persons), with clinical signs of a lesion of the central structures of the optic system (optic agnosia or hemianoptic visual field defects) the optical effect of psilocybine was present in only 24% as against B5'i of the patients with other brain lesions (this latter figure approximated closely the incidence of visual hallucinations in normal subjects). Differences in incidence of some qualitativelyUYUUUUIUUU different visual phenomena were also found. In patients with lesions of the central structures of the optic system, all components of the optic stimulation system were greatly reduced (entoptic phenomena; elementary and formed visions; complex (figurative and scenic) hallucinations and dysmegaloptic and dysmorphoptic disturbances), in comparison with the other 2 groups (normals and patients with other brain lesions). The group of patients with brain lesions not involving the central part of the optic system also differed from the normal subjects in that all qualities of the optic stimulation syndrome were reduced, but more especially the occurrence of complex hallucinations and of dysmorphopsias and dysmegalopsias. This is probably related to the weakened action of psychotoKiirnetic agent:s in organic brain disease. Other investigations are briefly mentioned, in which similar reductions of the effect of psychotomimetics have been noted in relation to nonvisual hallucinations and disturbances of time sense. The cause of the reduction of the optic stimulatory effect of psilocybine in patients with central lesions of the optic system is sought in a (purely hypothetical) supramaximal inhibition (in the sense of Vvedenskis parabiosis), or in a weakening of facilitatory corticofugal activity in the presence of such lesions.
