2600. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 288106195
T1  - Acid-base behavior of separated canine renal tubule cells
A1  - Struyvenberg A.
A1  - Morrison R.B.
A1  - Relman A.S. 
Y1  - 1968//
N2  - In separated canine renal tubules intracellular pH was calculated from the distribution of DMO-14C, using inulin as a measurement of noncellular water, At normal PCO2 and extracellular (HCO3-), renal cells were found to be more alkaline (mean pH 7.32) than most other mammalian cells. Cell acidity varied in direct linear relation to extracellular acidity when the latter was changed by systematic variation of either PCty or extracellular [HCO3-], but PCO2 had a significantly greater effect. Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase by addition of 10-3 M acetazolamide significantly increased cellular pH. Neither acute potassium depletion nor rapid repletion significantly affected cell pH. The results suggest that renal tubular cells are relatively alkaline because they secrete acid; the secretory process appears to be only indirectly linked to carbonic anhydrase.
