2604. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 287076723
T1  - A possible correlation between drug-induced hallucinations in man and a behavioural response in mice
A1  - Corne S.J.
A1  - Pickering R.W. 
Y1  - 1967//
N2  - The production of head twitches in mice appears to be a suitable laboratory test for predicting whether a compound is likely to be hallucinogenic in man. This is based on the following findings: With the exception of acetyllysergide and nalorphine, hallucinogenic drugs induced head twitches in mice. Chemically related compounds which are not hallucinogenic did not induce head twitches with the exception of 5-HTP, ergometrine, alpha-ethylmescaline and 3,4-dimethoxyphenethylamine (DMPE). It is suggested that these 4 drugs might produce hallucinations if they were given in high enough doses. There is a good correlation between drug-induced hallucinations in man and drug-induced head twitches in mice in terms of dose/kg. Chlorpromazine, haloperidol, tetrahydroaminacrine and MAO inhibitors can antagonize hallucinations in man. These same compounds antagonized head-twitches induced in mice by the appropriate hallucinogens. Cross-tolerance has been demonstrated between lysergide (LSD) and mescaline, LSD and psilocybin and LSD and DMT in man. Mescaline, psilocybin and DMT also antagonized LSD-induced head twitches in mice. A potent antiserotonin, methysergide, antagonized the head twitches induced by a wide variety of chemically different hallucinogens. This gives support to the idea that hallucinogens may act by mimicking, releasing or potentiating serotonin centrally.
