2616. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 286095836
T1  - Effects of psychomimetic drugs on EEG and conditioned behavior in rabbit
T3  - Effets de certains hallucinogenes sur l'electroencephalogramme et le comportement conditionne du lapin
A1  - Baran L.
A1  - Longo V.G. 
Y1  - 1965//
N2  - Behavioral and EEG effects of various psychomimetic drugs were studied in rabbit. Action on electrical activity and cardiovascular system was studied on acute preparations. On chronical preparations EEG effects were studied and compared with spontaneous behavior variations. On specially trained animals the influence of the drugs on a discrimination conditioned exercise was compared with EEG effects. The drugs studied were tryptamine, dimethyltryptamine, diethyltryptamine, bufotenine, psilocybine and mescaline. On the EEG all the drugs induced a disappearance of hippocampal (theta) rhythm. The most active drug was psilocybine, and the least one is mescaline, which induced modifications of the EEG only at doses of 75 mg./ kg. Generally, these drugs influence discrimination conditioned exercise with doses lower than those having an effect on the EEG. Analysis of effects on EEG and behavior confirmed that these drugs have their own characteristics, which distinguish them from other drugs (sympathomimetic amines, analeptics, convulsants) and which allow them to be classified in the same class as lysergide (LSD).
