2627. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 281495835
T1  - Pharmacological characterization and detection of psy-chotomimetic agents. ii. morphine antagonism in mice
T3  - Caracterisation et detection pharraacologiques des substances hallucinogenes. ii. antagonismes vis-a-vis de la morphine chez la souris
A1  - Jacob J.
A1  - Loiseau G.
A1  - Echinard- Garin P.
A1  - Barthelemy C.
A1  - Lafille C. 
Y1  - 1964//
N2  - Effects of psychotomimetic drugs on morphinized mice were examined by the hotplate method. The drugs were administered s. c. 30 min. before morphine and 1 hr. before the first exposure. Two reactions were studied, (a) A simple one: licking at the first exposure (dose of morphine 10 mg. /kg., s. c. ) and (b) a more elaborate one: jumping at the 3rd exposure (dose of morphine 1 mg. /kg. s.c. ). Effects varied with the drug, dosage and reaction. For lysergide, bromolysergide, psilocybine, dimethyl- and diethyltryptamines, nalorphine, and mescaline, the main effect was shortening of the licking times and often, for similar or higher doses, of the jumping times. The potencies in reducing the effect of morphine on licking correlated cept for nalorphine which induces hypothermia, with their hyperthermic activities in the rabbit, as determined previously (Jacob and Lafille). 'JB 318' and 'JB 336', two piperidyl diphenylglycolates, also reduced reaction times of morphinized mice but they differed from the aforementioned compounds in being more potent on the 'jumping' than on the 'licking' reaction, a property shared also by scopolamine. Dinitrophenol and pentetrazole were almost ineffective whereas DL-amphetamine and phencyclidine further increased the reaction times. Mechanisms of action and value of these 2 tests for screening and characterization of psychotomimetic agents are discussed.
