2631. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 280973719
T1  - PHARMACOLOGICAL DETECTION and CHARACTERIZATION of HALLUCINOGENS. I. HYPERTHERMIZING ACTIVITIES in THE RABBIT
T3  - Caracterisation et detection pharmacologiques des substances hallucinogenes. I. Activites hyperthermisantes chez le lapin
A1  - Jacob J.
A1  - Lafille C. 
Y1  - 1963//
N2  - Hyperthermic actions together with their time and dose effect relationships have been determined in rabbits for different hallucinogenic agents, either of the essential type (lysergide, psilocybine, mescaline, dimethyl- and diethyltryptamines, N-ethyl-3-piperidyl benzilate ('JB 318') and an analogue ('JB 336'), nalorphine and phencyclidine) or of the contingent type (bromolysergide, alpha-ethyltryptamine, amphetamine). Pentetrazole and dinitrophenol have also been studied. All the hallucinogenic s studied raised the temperature, the only exception being nalorphine. Effects increased regularly with dosages except for bromolysergide whose action, on the contrary, subsided, and for phencyclidine. No characteristic permits, without further study, to distinguish between the action of hallucinogenic and that of dinitrophenol. Hyperthermic activity does thus not appear to be a necessary or sufficient characteristic of all hallucinogenic agents. Nevertheless, a fair quantitative correlation has been found between both activities, for compounds with actual or potential indole structure: lysergide, bromolysergide, psilocybine, dimethyl- and diethyltryptamines and mescaline. The 2 piperidyl benzilates ('JB 318' and 'JB 336') behave, in both activities, as intermediates between the tryptamines and mescaline. However, their position on the correlation diaphragm discloses a relatively too low hyperthermic activity; this suggests a possible different modality of hallucinogenic action. Mydriatic activity did not correlate with the other 2, as mescaline was, on the contrary, found to be miotic. With due allowance for aberrant actions (negative with nalorphine, positive with dinitrophenol) the test of hyperthermia appears thus as one of a battery for preclinical detection and even, in some chemical series, quantitative evaluation of hallucinogenic properties. Its advantages are: its ability to give information for both predominant action and side effect and its experimental simplicity, this latter being illustrated by a screening schedule already applied to series of synthetics. Other tests will be described in a following publication. (II, 8).
