2632. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 281099689
T1  - Effect of mescaline, lysergic acid diethylamide and psilocybin on color perception
A1  - Hartman A.M.
A1  - Hollister L.E. 
Y1  - 1963//
N2  - Twenty subjects were given 3 psychotomimetic drugs (mescaline, LSD-25 and psilocybin) in fixed doses, 18 receiving all 3. Colour experience was tested by applying stimuli involving colour perception, stimuli eliciting subjective colours, and stimuli usually devoid of visual experience. Almost all measures of colour perception were affected. Hue discrimination was decreased significantly by psilocybin, to a slightly lesser extent by the other two drugs. Colour reports in afterimages were significantly increased by all 3 drugs, but duration of after-image was increased significantly only by psilocybin. Elicitation of subjective colours from flicker was increased by all 3 drugs, significantly more by mescaline and LSD-25 than by psilocybin. The combination of flicker and pure tones evoked more colour reports than flicker alone. The increase was significant only after LSD-25, no significant differences being demonstrated between the effects of the 3 drugs. Visual effects, both colours and patterns, elicited by pure tones were signiiicantly increased by LSD and mescaline, though these effects were not great at cne doses used in this study. Spectral patterns of evoked colours varied slightly between the 3 drugs. These results are interpreted as indicating that stimuli which evoke subjective colour phenomena, or even those not usually associated with visual phenomena, are enhanced by psychotomimetics. On the other hand, the usual perception of colour, as judged by hue discrimination, may actually be slightly decreased.
