2633. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 281096197
T1  - Effects of chemical stimulation of electrically-induced phosphenes on their bandwidth, shape, number and intensity
A1  - Knoll M.
A1  - Kugler J.
A1  - Hofer O.
A1  - Lawder S.D. 
Y1  - 1963//
N2  - It has been shown in earlier work that 'phosphenes' (luminescent endogenous patterns experienced with closed eyes) may occur not only during drowsiness, shock, fasting, meditation, deprivation of sensory signals or chemical intoxication but also during and after stimulation of the brain from cranial electrodes by rectangular electric pulses mainly within the EEG frequency range (1 to 30 c./sec.). Because of their relatively narrow bandwidth, it was possible to distinguish up to 12 different electrically-induced patterns per individual. Furthermore, it has been found that the same individual may experience the same phosphene pattern at different stimulation frequencies. Particular patterns may remain stable for at least 6 months. For checking the effect of additional chemical excitation on phosphenes, if any, 2 'control' phosphene patterns of 2 subjects were followed up during a period of 6 months. There was considerable stability of their shape and of their mean excitation frequency. Sometimes the occurrence of one particular 'control' phosphene was found at 10 c./sec. and integers of this frequency. As a result of additional chemical excitation by mescaline, psilocybin and LSD (in one subject) the following effects were observed: (1) Temporary increase of the original 2 'control' patterns in intensity and bandwidth from +/- 13% up to 100%. (2) Production of a great number of new phosphene patterns or pictures (mostly with non-abstract shape) with large bandwith and intensity, disappearing in the first and last stages of chemical stimulation when switching off the additional electrical stimulation. (3) During electrical and maximum chemical stimulation the new drug-induced pictures prevailed and the 'control patterns' disappeared (observed with mescaline and psilocybin only). (4) If, during electrical stimulation, only the stimulation current is varied at a constant frequency, the shape of the induced patterns may vary. (5) If, during electrical-chemical stimulation, the bandwith is large (order of +/- 50% to 100%) the change of picture shape cannot be accomplished by changing the pulse current. On the assumption that each phosphene pattern is produced by a neuronal network with definable bandwith, a simple cybernetic model of phosphene generation can be devised. According to this model, a feedback or resonance effect exists if the follower of each applied pulse arrives at the input neuron of the chain not earlier than after the time tN, but at the latest after the time n-tN, where tN is the delay-time of one neuron, and 'n' an integer indicating the number of neurons in each particular chain. In contrast to models of 'objective' viewing, no inhibitory neuronal network elements are necessary. The realization of such a model is possible, for example, by substituting neurons with electromechanical relays which have switching delay times of the order of msec, ('coincidence circuit'). From the model it may also be concluded that the increase of excitation frequency bandwidth during additional chemical excitation may be due to the association of new chemically pre-excited neuronal chains (belonging to larger cortical fields) to the primary ensemble excitable by electrical stimulation only. This would be an analogue to Dusser de Barenne's and McCulloch's findings on the effect of strychninization of brain tissue.
