2644. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 281095596
T1  - Search for experimental equivalents of hallucinogenic activity. Effects of hallucinogens on rectal temperature in rabbit and on morphine effects in mouse
T3  - Recherche d'equivalents experimentaux au pouvoir hallucinogene. Action des hallucinogenes sur la temperature rectale du Lapin et sur les effets de la morphine chez la souris
A1  - Jacob J.
A1  - Lafille C.
A1  - Loiseau G.
A1  - Echinard-Garin P.
A1  - Barthelemy C. 
Y1  - 1962//
N2  - With lysergamide, bromolysergamide, psilocybin, dimethyl- and diethyltryptamines and mescaline, a satisfactory correlation exists between temperature raising (in rabbits), antianalgesic (in mice) and hallucinogenic (in men, according to the literature) potencies. The piperidyl diphenylglycolates were also found to raise the temperature in the rabbit but their hallucinogenic potencies were seemingly in closer relationship with a particular antagonism towards morphine in mice. For nalorphine - which which induces hypothermia in the rabbit - antianalgesic and hallucinogenic activities are in the same relationship as for the above-mentioned substances; amphetamine, and phencyclidine raised the rectal temperature of the rabbit but did not antagonize morphine. The results are discussed with regard to the catecholergic, tryptamine and cholinolytic hypotheses of mode of action of hallucinogenic drugs, and the value of the tests as screening and characterizing methods for such agents.
