2645. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 281076977
T1  - Drug-induced psychoses and schizophrenic reactions: A critical comparison
A1  - Hollister L.E. 
Y1  - 1962//
N2  - Comparison of clinical syndromes from psychotomimetic drugs with schizophrenic reactions reveals some basic differences in particular symptoms but, most of all, in sum. Screened normal subjects given fixed doses of each of three psychotomimetics (mescaline, psilocybin, and LSD-25), using blind controls, reported a preponderance of somatic and perceptual effects and comparatively few psychopathologic symptoms of the type evident in schizophrenics. Dizziness, weakness, drowsiness, tremulousness, paraesthesias, and blurred vision were common, as were visual illusions and hallucinations. Disorientation, paranoid ideation, disturbed thinking, and auditory, gustatory, olfactory or tactile hallucinations were uncommon. With JB-329, a different picture was observed, characteristic of a toxic delirium. Another basis for comparing drug-induced psychoses and schizophrenic reactions was by means of brief taped interviews with 6 subjects under drugs (2 each on psilocybin, mescaline, and JB-329) and 6 schizophrenics. Using imperfect blind controls, 78 professional raters were able to judge the drug subjects correctly from 67 to 73 times, the schizophrenics correctly from 66 to 75 times. Thus it would appear that a reasonably experienced clinical observer should easily be able to distinguish the 2 conditions. In view of these considerable clinical differences between drug states and the naturally occurring psychosis, questions have been raised about the relevance of the former to the study of the latter.
