2649. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 281236429
T1  - The effect of psilocybin upon the systemic, pulmonary and coronary circulation of the intact dog
A1  - Maxwell G.M.
A1  - Kneebone G.M.
A1  - Elliott R.B. 
Y1  - 1962//
N2  - Circulatory effects of a known paychotomimetic agent, peilocybin, were investigated. In anaesthetized dogs psilocybin in a dosage of 0.5 mg./kg. intravenously, depresses respiration and arterial O2 saturation, possibly deepening the anaesthesia. Psilocybin is a sympathomimetic agent, and raises blood pressure, heart rate, and pulmonary and peripheral vascular resistance. Coronary resistance remains unchanged, while blood flow increases significantly owing to the raised blood pressure. The efficiency of the heart becomes somewhat diminished. Cardiovascular effects of psilocybin are similar to those of serotonin, this being in accordance with the weak anti-serotonin effect of psilocybin.
