2656. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 281494011
T1  - Clinical and polygraphic study of a new type of psychodysleptic: 'Ditran'
T3  - Etude clinique et polygraphique d'un nouveau type de psycho-dysleptique: Le ditran
A1  - Delay J.
A1  - Deniker P.
A1  - Verdeaux G.
A1  - Ginestet D.
A1  - Peron P. 
Y1  - 1962//
N2  - This is an entirely synthetic product. It causes a remarkably constant syndrome in all subjects. After slow i. v. injection 4 stages can be distinguished. (1) Immediately after the injection autonomie disturbances develop, in the first place tachycardia and respiratory disorders. The duration of these symptoms is rather constant; they rapidly disappear after half an hour. (2) Very early - after a latency period of 10-20 min. - pyramidal symptoms develop. These persist for several hours. (3) After a phase of profound obnubilation, confusion and oneirism develop. These appear at the same time as the first autonomie phenomena and may persist in the form of an oneiric delirium for 12-24 hr. (4) A change in mood, viz. an expansive post-oneiric euphoria. The dysleptic and autonomic effects of ditran do not appear to be influenced by previous neuroleptic treatment. Tetrahydro-aminoacridine has a potent and immediate effect on the syndrome induced by ditran. This effect was verified by polygraphy and mainly involved the autonomic changes and the state of wakefulness. The effect of ditran thus differs from that of other dysleptics in current use. The severity of the somatic changes it produces resembles shock therapy. Similar effects have been observed by Sigwald after the administration of a phenothiazine derivative (7369 RP). The relatively poor oneiric experience contrasts with the great variety of sensations induced by mescaline, psilocybin and LSD 25.
