2667. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 281061053
T1  - Some observations on psilocybin, a new hallucinogen, in volunteer subjects
A1  - Malitz S.
A1  - Esecover H.
A1  - Wilkens B.
A1  - Hoch P.H. 
Y1  - 1960//
N2  - Fourteen paid volunteers, screened by clinical interviews, received psilocybin orally over a dosage range of 8 to 36 mg. The perceptual, affective, cognitive, behavioural and autonomic responses observed were qualitatively similar to those previously described with D-LSD 25, but quantitatively, a greater impairment of cognition with psilocybin was noted which may have been related either to dosage or to personality differences. EEG findings in 4 out of 8 subjects were similar to those previously reported with D-LSD 25 and mescaline. The use of psychotomimetic drugs as therapeutic agents in an out-patient setting may prove to be potentially hazardous. Acute stages of excitement, depression with suicidal ideation and impairment of judgment may occur with these drugs, even in the most carefully supervised in-patient setting. With out-patients, the danger of self-administration and inadequate supervision could combine and increase such occurrences. Further research is indicated regarding the site of action of hallucinogens, the relationship between personality, dosage level and total behavioural pattern, and the possible prognostic value of such drugs in assessing ego strength in specific individuals.
