2681. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 289046023
T1  - Differentiation of EEG deactivation
A1  - Kakolewski J.W. 
N2  - The 'deactivation' of the EEG evoked by prolonged acoustic stimuli is different from the 'deactivation' evoked by prolonged nociceptive stimuli. During prolonged acoustic stimulation the EEG arousal response to superimposed short acoustic stimulation is diminished, but enhanced to superimposed short nociceptive stimuli. During prolonged nociceptive stimulation the EEG arousal response is diminished to short superimposed acoustic and diminished or blocked to superimposed short nociceptive stimulation. Prolonged stimulation was able to 'deactivate' the EEG arousal evoked by nicotine, but not LSD-25 and psilocybin.
