2692. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 2018-40728-001
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/09540261.2018.1481827
T1  - Psychedelics as anti-inflammatory agents.
A1  - Flanagan, Thomas W
A1  - Nichols, Charles D
A2  - Adcock, Ahlenius, Akiyoshi, Allen, Alpini, Arzt, Aune, Barnes, Barnes, Bilbo, Booth, Brattelid, Brower, Bruhn, Busse, Carhart-Harris, Carhart-Harris, Carhart-Harris, Carhart-Harris, Carhart-Harris, Chaplin, Choi, Chung, Cloez-Tayarani, Cogolludo, Cosio, Darmon, Darveaux, Davydova, De Bie, Deckers, Derkach, Domeney, Drews, Durk, Egermayer, Filip, Franco-Molina, Fredriksson, Furtado, Fuxe, Ghia, Giulietti, Godfrey, Gooz, Graham, Griffiths, Gunawardhana, Hamid, Harbuz, Harbuz, Heffter, Hekking, Herr, Hey, Hill, Holloway, Hong, Hoyer, Inoue, Ito, Ito, Janeway, Jeffery, Johnson, Jouvet, Kang, Kenakin, Kenakin, Khan, Kim, Kubera, Kyzar, Laguzzi, Leon-Ponte, Locke, Mabry, Marconi, Margolis, Mason, McAlear, McBride, McCorvy, McKenna, McKune, Medzhitov, Meltzer, Meyerson, Mikulski, Miller, Miller, Moore, Morales-Garcia, Moreira, Nagatomo, Naik, Najjar, Nakae, Nau, Nau, Nichols, Nichols, Nichols, Nichols, Nichols, Nocito, Osmond, Overington, Palczewski, Pellegrino, Pelletier, Peretz, Pierce, Politis, Quirce, Radtke, Ray, Raymond, Ren, Ross, Rossi, Roth, Roth, Ruddell, Samson, Shah, Shajib, Shulgin, Shulgin, Smith, Soga, Sonda, Sonier, Sparkes, Stefulj, Steyn, Strawbridge, Thiebot, Urban, Vollenweider, Wacker, Warrington, Welsh, Williams, Yu
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT)2A receptor agonists have recently emerged as promising new treatment options for a variety of disorders. The recent success of these agonists, also known as psychedelics, like psilocybin for the treatment of anxiety, depression, obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), and addiction, has ushered in a renaissance in the way these compounds are perceived in the medical community and populace at large. One emerging therapeutic area that holds significant promise is their use as anti-inflammatory agents. Activation of 5-HT2A receptors produces potent anti-inflammatory effects in animal models of human inflammatory disorders at sub-behavioural levels. This review discusses the role of the 5-HT2A receptor in the inflammatory response, as well as highlight studies using the 5-HT2A agonist (R)-2,5-dimethoxy-4-iodoamphetamine [(R)-DOI] to treat inflammation in cellular and animal models. It also examines potential mechanisms by which 5-HT2A agonists produce their therapeutic effects. Overall, psychedelics regulate inflammatory pathways via novel mechanisms, and may represent a new and exciting treatment strategy for several inflammatory disorders. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
