2750. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 2015-46616-029
T1  - The mystical potential of lucid dreaming.
T2  - Lucid dreaming: New perspectives on consciousness in sleep: Science, psychology, and education; Religion, creativity, and culture., Vols. 1-2
T3  - Practical and applied psychology.
A1  - Kelzer, Kenneth
A2  - Barks, Bly, Bly, Castaneda, Evans-Wentz, Garfield, Garfield, Green, Huxley, Jung, Jung, Kelzer, Kelzer, Kornfield, Krishna, LaBerge, Sinetar, Sparrow, Watts
A3  - Hurd, Ryan [Ed], Bulkeley, Kelly [Ed]
Y1  - 2014//
N2  - Today there is a worldwide growing interest in the reporting of mystical experiences. This is due to some extent to the psychedelic revolution launched in the 1960s, when many people reported having mystical experiences while under the influence of LSD, psilocybin, mescaline, and other psychedelic substances. In the wake of that era, as I was first beginning to speak and write about my "experiment" with lucid dreaming, a colleague and close friend of mine said something I have never forgotten: it is difficult to talk about mystical experiences or even lucid dreams to people who have not had either. My colleague believed that some people could easily become angry upon hearing what I have to say, insofar as they make no claims to having mystical experiences themselves and could easily feel alienated. Even so, he encouraged me to communicate them anyway. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
