2838. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 2013-31198-012
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.1503/jpn.130109
T1  - Antidepressant effects of botulinum toxin A: Scientific rationale: Author's response.
T3  - Psychiatric Journal of the University of Ottawa
A1  - Young, Simon N
AI  - ORCID: http://orcid.org/Young, Simon N.; ORCID: http://orcid.org/0000-0001-9666-8096
A2  - Finzi, Young
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Replies to comments by Eric Finzi (see record 2013-31198-011) on the original article by Simon N. Young (see record 2013-06564-002) regarding the antidepressant effects of ketamine and botulinum toxin and the anxiolytic effect of psilocybin. The present author contests Finzi's assertion that a "very significant omission" was present in the original article; that Young did not mention a study by Finzi and Wasserman that was "the first to show that botulinum toxin A could be used as an antidepressant." The present author asserts that the theory that resulted in the test of botulinum toxin for the treatment of depression, the facial feedback theory, predates the case series of Finzi and Wasserman. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
