2839. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 2013-31198-011
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.1503/jpn.130090
T1  - Antidepressant effects of botulinum toxin A: Scientific rationale.
T3  - Psychiatric Journal of the University of Ottawa
A1  - Finzi, Eric
A2  - Finzi, Finzi, Finzi, Wollmer, Young, Young
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Comments on an article by Simon N. Young (see record 2013-06564-002). The author redress a very significant omission in a recent publication by Young. The original author states that the facial feedback literature "provided the rationale for a recent trial by Wollmer and colleagues studying the effect of injecting Botulinum toxin into 3 sites in the glabella region of depressed patients." This statement completely ignores the study by my group, published 6 years before the Wollmer study. We were the first to show that Botulinum toxin A could be used as an antidepressant by inhibiting frowning in patients with major depression. This initial study has provided the rationale for future trials of Botulinum toxin A in populations with depression. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
