Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Reviews the book, The Psychedelic Renaissance: Reassessing the Role of Psychedelic Drugs in 21st Century Psychiatry and Society by Ben Sessa (see record 2012-23435-000). The book presents the best overview to date of the state of the science of psychedelic research. While recreational use of drugs such as LSD and psilocybin continues, illegal and unabated, human subject research has resumed, shedding new light on aspects of neuropharmacology, mental illness, and healing. Reacquainting oneself with psychedelic drug use and research is important for any psychologist seeking to stay abreast of our current understanding of the brain, the mind, and the myriad interactions between them. The Psychedelic Renaissance includes access to accurate information that can maximize harm reduction and alert clinicians (and legislators) to the actual dangers and benefits of the numerous licit and illicit substances. Because restrictions on research, although lessened in recent years, are still formidable, The Psychedelic Renaissance provides a much-needed framework for the current understanding of psychedelic drugs, current and future research, and their relation to the human condition. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
