2927. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 2007-11123-010
T1  - A psychoanalytic approach to psychedelic psychotherapy.
T2  - Psychedelic medicine: New evidence for hallucinogenic substances as treatments., Vol. 2
A1  - Merkur, Dan
A2  - Abramson, Abramson, Abramson, Abramson, Arendsen-Hein, Arendsen-Hein, Baastians, Bakan, Balint, Balint, Bion, Bion, Bishop, Blumenthal, Blumenthal, Blumenthal, Breslaw, Buckman, Buckman, Buckman, Buckman, Buckman, Cohen, Cohen, Cohen, Dahlberg, Dahlberg, Dahlberg, Eisner, Eisner, Fakhry, Frank, Freud, Gill, Grof, Grof, Grof, Grof, Grof, Grotstein, Grotstein, Grotstein, Harman, Hausner, De-Nur, Kohut, Krippner, Loewald, Loewald, Maslow, Maslow, Maslow, Masters, Mechaneck, Merkur, Merlan, Mosher, Pahnke, Rhead, Rosenfeld, Rosenfeld, Sandison, Searles, Segal, Soskin, Unger, Winnicott, Winnicott, Wolf
A3  - Winkelman, Michael J [Ed], Roberts, Thomas B [Ed]
Y1  - 2007//
N2  - Sandison introduced the term psycholytic, "mind-loosening," in 1960 to designate the use of LSD (lysergic acid diethylamide), mescaline, and psilocybin as adjuvants to existing psychotherapies. The doses were small enough (25 to 150 micrograms of LSD) that patients were able to converse throughout their sessions. For the therapists, psycholytic therapy was business as usual with a patient who sometimes was high. Osmond coined the term psychedelic, "mind-expanding," in 1957 to designate the mystical experiences that tend to occur at higher dosage levels (200 and more micrograms of LSD). Originally a chemotherapy, psychedelic therapy was soon modified through the addition of several weeks of intensive psychotherapy prior to the psychedelic experience, and debriefing for a few days afterward. For the most part, psycholytic approaches were favored in Europe, psychedelic approaches in America. In the 1970s, researchers at the Maryland Psychiatric Research Center attempted to integrate the two procedures. A therapy that used psychedelics in addition to interpretation was, by definition, incapable of being psychoanalytic; and psychoanalysts largely ignored clinical work with psychedelics. Thirty and more years later, the Freudian model that was inadequate to the explanation of high-dose psychedelic experiences is itself largely passe. The clinical challenge for psychoanalytic psychedelic psychotherapists is to learn what to say to patients at high-dosage levels that produce intensifications of the rapport in fashions consistent with Grof's clinical experiences at low-dosage levels. Talking about oral, anal, and Oedipal symbolism, ego psychology's defense mechanisms, and Kleinian theory's internal objects are all ill-advised. The task is to interpret what a patient's unconscious integrative process is trying to communicate to consciousness. For present purposes, the varieties of psychedelic experiences will be divided into six categories: apperceptual, neurotic, narrative fantasy, psychotomimetic, creative, and unitive phenomena. I will discuss interventions appropriate to each category in turn. The fine-tuning of my proposals, through the discovery of precise interpretations that are most helpful clinically, can only be made through trial-and-error in the course of clinical work with individual patients. Unfortunately, for political reasons methodologically normative clinical research is presently illegal. This chapter accordingly proposes a variety of interventions that may currently be used lawfully to guide emergency work with people who have engaged in illicit self-experimentation. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
