TY  - JOUR
ID  - 2005-12236-016
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.eurpsy.2005.04.008
T1  - Hallucinogen persisting perception disorder after psilocybin consumption: A case study.
T3  - Psychiatrie & Psychobiologie
A1  - Espiard, Marie-Laure
A1  - Lecardeur, Laurent
A1  - Abadie, Pascale
A1  - Halbecq, Isabelle
A1  - Dollfus, Sonia
A2  - Abraham, Aldurra, Halpern, Horowitz, Lerner, Niveau, Pierrot, Vollenweider, Young
Y1  - 2005//
N2  - The recurrence of flashbacks without acute or chronic hallucinogen consumption has been recognized in the DSM IV criteria as the hallucinogen persisting perception disorder (HPPD), Perceptual disturbances may last for 5 years or more and represent a real psychosocial distress. We reported here a case of a 18-year-old young man presenting HPPD after a mixed intoxication with psylocibin and cannabis. This report shows symptomatic recurrences persisting more than 8 months. Various differential diagnoses were evoked and our therapeutic strategies were described. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
