2962. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 2002-06413-011
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/02791072.2002.10399970
T1  - "Mushroom madness" in the Papua New Guinea Highlands: A case of nicotine poisoning?
T3  - Journal of Psychedelic Drugs
A1  - Thomas, Benjamin
A2  - Clarke, Dobkin de Rios, Emboden, Gitlow, Heim, Heim, Heim, Heim, Heim, Lewin, Marshall, Reay, Reay, Reay, Reay, Ross, Shaw, Singer, Vicedom, Wasson, Wilbert, Wilbert
Y1  - 2002//
N2  - "Mushroom madness" is a condition that was first reported in the Western Highlands Province of Papua New Guinea. This condition was originally blamed on the ingestion of hallucinogenic mushrooms. This was later proven to be incorrect and this condition was explained instead as a form of collective hysteria. There is, however, ethnographic, phytochemical and pharmacological evidence to suggest that "mushroom madness" was actually a state of acute nicotine poisoning produced by the ingestion of toxic quantities of tobacco. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
