2969. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 2002-10231-005
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0361-9230%2801%2900646-3
T1  - A systems model of altered consciousness: Integrating natural and drug-induced psychoses.
T2  - Special Issue: Serotonin: From the molecule to the clinic.
A1  - Vollenweider, Franz X
A1  - Geyer, Mark A
A2  - Aghajanian, Aghajanian, Alexander, Andreasen, Andreasen, Anis, Anwyl, Arora, Asanuma, Asanuma, Bakshi, Bakshi, Bleuler, Bowers, Bowers, Braff, Braff, Braff, Breier, Brodie, Buchsbaum, Buchsbaum, Carlsson, Catafau, Chapman, Cleghorn, Coenen, Coenen, Collier, Crick, Deakin, Dean, DeLisi, Dittrich, Dittrich, Dittrich, Dittrich, Early, Early, Ebmeier, Fischer, Fischman, Freedman, Fuster, Gaddum, Geyer, Geyer, Geyer, Geyer, Geyer, Geyer, Geyer, Goddard, Gouzoulis, Gouzoulis-Mayfrank, Gouzoulis-Mayfrank, Gouzoulis-Mayfrank, Grimwood, Gurevich, Heckers, Heimann, Hermle, Hermle, Hermle, Hermle, Hernegger, Ishimaru, Jentsch, Joyce, Karper, Karper, Keeler, Keith, Kim, Kornhuber, Krebs-Thomson, Krystal, Krystal, Krystal, Lahti, Laruelle, Leuner, Liddle, Lieberman, Liu, Malhotra, Malhotra, Malhotra, Mansbach, Marek, Martindale, McCabe, McCann, McGhie, Meltzer, Meltzer, Meltzer, Milner, Milner, Moghaddam, Mordenti, Moruzzi, Olney, Perry, Posner, Pribram, Reneman, Rothman, Rummele, Sabri, Scharfetter, Scharfetter, Scharfetter, Scharfetter, Schreckenberger, Schroder, Sheppard, Sherman, Sherman, Sherman, Siegel, Silbersweig, Singer, Sipes, Sipes, Smythies, Spreafico, Steriade, Steriade, Steriade, Steriade, Swerdlow, Swerdlow, Swerdlow, Swerdlow, Swerdlow, Swerdlow, Swerdlow, Thatcher, Titeler, Varty, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Vollenweider, Weber, Weike, Wolkin, Wooley
Y1  - 2001//
N2  - Reviews recent studies assessing commonalities among naturally occurring and drug-induced psychoses, and presents a model. Evidence from neuroimaging and behavioral studies suggests that functional disturbances within cortico-striato-thalamic pathways are critical to psychotic symptom formation in drug-induced and possibly also naturally occurring psychoses. Results of studies examining psychotomimetic drugs, including the N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) glutamate receptor antagonist ketamine and the 5-hydroxytryptamine (5-HT) type 2A receptor agonist psilocybin, suggest that the hallucinogenic effects of these drugs arise from their common capacity to disrupt thalamo-cortical gating of external and internal information to the cortex. Deficient gating of sensory and cognitive information results in an overloading inundation of information and subsequent cognitive fragmentation and psychosis. It is concluded that serotonergic hallucinogens and NMDA antagonists produce psychological changes that resemble the symptoms characteristics of incipient stages of schizophrenic psychoses. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
