2971. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 2001-95016-352
T1  - Interactions between serotonergic (5-HT2) hallucinogens and NMDA antagonists.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - West, William Britton
Y1  - 2001//
N2  - PCP-like drugs are noncompetitive NMDA antagonists that produce both anesthesia and neuronal damage, presumably by binding to a "PCP-site" on the NMDA receptor complex. LSD-like drugs are classic hallucinogens that produce their central effects by acting as agonists at 5-HT2A and 5-HT2C receptors. Despite these differences, recent research is suggests that an interaction between glutamatergic and serotonergic receptors may explain the varied effects of these substances, as well as be of relevance to the etiology of schizophrenia. PCP-induced hypofunction of the NMDA receptor has been proposed to explain the actions of PCP-like drugs and the interaction of these substances with LSD-like drugs. Thus far, however, few behavioral studies involving combination tests with PCP and LSD have been performed to verify the utility and accuracy of this hypothesis. Therefore, additional behavioral experiments involving dug discrimination and audiogenic seizure behavior were conucted as part of this project. The purpose of the present study was to: (1) determine the effect other hallucinogens such as mescaline and psilocybin have on the PCP-cue and to (2) test the ability of LSD to block the neurotoxic effects of PCP as indicated by heat shock protein expression, a measure thought to be superior to vacuolar damage. PCP given to adult rats that have been previously exposed to a priming noise will reduce audiogenic seizure behavior. However when PCP is given to rats on post-natal day (PND) 14, and subsequently tested on PND 32, audiogenic seizure behavior increases. The third purpose of this experiment was to determine if LSD could in fact block the PCP-induced increase of audiogenic seizure behavior. The results of the present series of experiments indicate that LSD-like drugs can effectively block both behavioral and neurotoxic effects of PCP. In addition, LSD blocked the increased incidence and duration of wild running behavior that are seen following PCP (10 mg/kg). LSD was also able to block the expression of heat shock protein in the cingulate and retrosplenial cortices following high doses of PCP (50 mg/kg). These findings are in direct support of current theories that propose a common mechanism underlying all hallucinogenic. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
