T1  - Patterns of recreational drug use at dance events in Edinburgh, Scotland.
T3  - British Journal of Addiction
A1  - Riley, Sarah C. E
A1  - James, Charlotte
A1  - Gregory, Danielle
A1  - Dingle, Henry
A1  - Cadger, Mike
A2  - Barnard, Bean, Branigan, Calafat, Christophorou, Collin, Dalgarno, Ditton, Dorn, Fawcett, Forsyth, Hammersley, Hayes, Henry, Jansen, Johnson, Leitner, Lenton, Macdonald, Measham, Morrison, Mulland, Parker, Parker, Pearson, Pedersen, Ramsay, Saunders, Shewan, Shewan, Shewan, Shiner, Shiner, Stevens, Thornton, Ward, Wareing
Y1  - 2001//
N2  - Examined the patterns of drug use at dance (rave) events in terms of prevalence, frequency, type of drugs used, patterns of use, access and risk-associated behaviours. 122 drug users (57% males, 43% females), 90% of whom were in employment or education, with an age range of 16-47 yrs, participated. Over 80% of the participants had used ecstasy and amphetamine, over 30% cocaine and LSD; over 10% nitrites, psilocybin and ketamine and less than 5% had used crack or tranquillizers. Participants reported regular consumption of ecstasy and amphetamine (e.g., 35% used ecstasy and 25% amphetamine on a weekly basis) often taken in combination, with the occasional use of cocaine, LSD, ketamine and psilocybin. Poly- and mixing-drug behaviours were significantly more likely than monodrug usage. Drugs were accessed through friends than from any other source. Eighty-five per cent reported mixing drugs and/or alcohol, 35% driving on drugs, 36% having a bad experience on drugs; 30% unprotected sex; and 0.9% injecting drugs. Women in the sample reported higher consumption than men. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
