2983. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 1998-03402-010
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.1097/00008877-199811000-00011
T1  - Effects of the hallucinogen psilocybin on habituation and prepulse inhibition of the startle reflex in humans.
T2  - Special Issue: Human behavioural pharmacology
A1  - Gouzoulis-Mayfrank, E
A1  - Heekeren, K
A1  - Thelen, B
A1  - Lindenblatt, H
A1  - Kovar, K.-A
A1  - Sass, H
A1  - Geyer, M. A
Y1  - 1998//
N2  - Hallucinogenic drugs have been shown to disrupt prepulse inhibition (PPI) and/or retard habituation of the startle reflex in animal models of schizophrenia, consistent with the view of hallucinogen-induced states as "model psychoses." The present authors evaluated the effects of the hallucinogen psilocybin on PPI and habituation of the startle reflex in a double-blind, placebo-controlled human study with 12 healthy 27-45 yr olds. Psilocybin increased PPI and had no clear effect on habituation in this small human sample, in contrast to results from animal studies. These findings must be considered preliminary because several factors, including dose regimens and experimental parameters, may influence the results of studies on startle plasticity. Further investigations both with psychotic patients in different stages of the disease and with human and animal models of schizophrenia are needed to explore the effects of hallucinogens on sensorimotor gating and the relationship between information processing in hallucinogenic drug-induced states and the naturally occurring psychoses. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
