2985. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 1998-07149-011
T1  - Good trips, bad trips: Psychedelic drugs and dissociation.
T2  - Broken images, broken selves: Dissociative narratives in clinical practice.
A1  - Greenberg, Gary
A3  - Krippner, Stanley [Ed], Powers, Susan Marie [Ed]
Y1  - 1997//
N2  - Psychedelic experience is inherently dissociative, a journey to psychic places unknown. Some trips are terrifying, others ecstatic, but many of them offer unique opportunities for therapeutic exploration and growth. This chapter relates 2 "travelogues" of people who used psilocybin mushrooms. One trip was frightening and unpleasant, the other joyful. But both became an integral part of therapy and had an important influence on the experiencers' lives. A brief account of the history of psychedelic drug use in clinical practice is provided to help set the stage for a discussion of therapeutic interventions with psychedelic drug users. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
