2986. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 1997-95004-261
T1  - Listening for the logos: A study of reports of audible voices at high doses of psilocybin.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Beach, Horace Joseph
Y1  - 1997//
N2  - There are reports that psilocybin mushrooms can engender a dialogue between an imbiber and a voice of unknown origin. The objective of the present study was to search for such reports. An attempt was made to look for differences between those who reported having heard a voice with psilocybin use and those who had not, and to characterize the voice. An anonymous questionnaire was distributed among the members of several organizations resulting in a sample of 128 participants. The study demonstrated that the phenomenon of a perceived voice during psilocybin mushroom use was reported in better than a third of participants. The Yes voice(s) group reported having taken psilocybin more times, a larger average amount per use, more often taking mushrooms they grew, more times trying to hear voices, and more often in darkness, than the No voice(s) group. The most statistically significant differences were that the Yes group took psilocybin more often when they were alone, and more often reported hearing voices with other drugs. No statistically significant differences between the groups were found on the limited measures of personality assessed, nor were sex differences discovered. In this study, therefore, individual characteristics were found to be unrelated to the hearing of voices. The modal voice tended to possess a number of features: it sounded like an old basso male, who spoke slow and softly. The voice was often reported to express emotion. Generally, the voice was found to be singular and last for only a few minutes. The experience of hearing a voice was described as positive, insightful, useful, clear-sounding, and sensible. Also it was reported that one could communicate with the voice and gain information from the encounter. A sense of "otherness" was also frequently reported, and the voice speaking from this position of otherness was statistically significantly correlated with the participant reporting having gained insight. Finally, the abundance of reported n (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
