2992. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 1994-41512-001
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/07448481.1994.9936359
T1  - Increasing use of "Ecstasy" (MDMA) and other hallucinogens on a college campus.
T3  - Journal of the American College Health Association
A1  - Cuomo, Michael J
A1  - Dyment, Paul G
A1  - Gammino, Victoria M
Y1  - 1994//
N2  - Conducted a random survey of illicit drug use by undergraduate students at a private southern university in 1990 and compared the results with results from a similar 1986 survey of that college's student population. During the 4 yrs since the 1st study, the prevalence of cocaine use declined from 39% to 21%, and use of traditional amphetamines declined from 22% to 12%. No significant differences were found in the use of marijuana (68% in 1986, 64% in 1990) or in use of LSD (14% in 1986, 17% in 1990). The use of mescaline/psilocybin increased from 8% to 24% and the use of MDMA increased from 16% to 24%. Mescaline/psilocybin and Ecstasy were more likely than the other drugs to have been used first during the students' college years, according to the 1990 study. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
