2993. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 1994-09930-001
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.3109/00952999309001618
T1  - Toward a comparative overview of dependence potential and acute toxicity of psychoactive substances used nonmedically.
A1  - Gable, Robert S
Y1  - 1993//
N2  - Compared dependence potential and acute toxicity across 20 abused psychoactive substances. A literature review was conducted that focused on 20 psychoactive substances. Six major drug classes were represented: anesthetics, cannabis, depressants/sedative-hypnotics, hallucinogens/psychedelics, opiates, and stimulants. Summary data were sent over a 2-yr period to an ad hoc panel of 35 toxicologists and psychopharmacologists, who were asked to correct and comment on the findings. Tentative results suggested that the margin of safety ("therapeutic index") varied between substances. Intravenous heroin appeared to have the greatest risk of dependence and acute lethality; oral psilocybin appeared to have the least. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
