2999. 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - 1989-08875-001
DO  - http://dx.doi.org/10.2190/GQ0B-4CBX-BB15-M6LH
T1  - "Natural highs" in an historical and biological context.
A1  - Emboden, William A
Y1  - 1988//
N2  - Discusses historical and contemporary patterns of substance use and abuse and suggests that it is an error to regard psychoactive natural substances (e.g., psilocybin-containing mushrooms) as reasonably safe. The literature on drug-induced ecstasies among diverse peoples produces overconfidence in the safety of inducing altered states by means of natural chemicals. Given the current level of experimentation, the lack of a context for use, and the lack of knowledge of the toxicity of many plant sources, it is argued that the use of these substances presents serious problems. Evidence of recent changes in patterns of experimentation with natural drugs suggests that the naive user may experience an unwarranted level of confidence. Genera and species of plants that produce psychoactive effects are presented. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
